GUI modernization, Phase 4 (fifth and final slice): Rust connection
errors and the Go capability `reason` become {code, params,
technical_detail} - the last item in ADR 0004's original Decision
section. Closes Phase 4.

## 1. Rust connection errors (desktop/src-tauri/src/agent.rs)

Twelve hard-coded English `Result<_, String>` messages across
`get`/`get_inner`/`connect` (both platforms) and `lib.rs`'s
`agent_get`/`race_cancellable` - confirmed by reading the whole file
before writing anything, not assumed from the earlier baseline survey.
`AgentError { code, params, technical_detail }` (new, `#[derive(Serialize)]`)
replaces every one of them; `technical_detail` is the exact sentence
this crate always produced, so nothing about what a raw-detail viewer
sees is lost.

Codes are named constants (`agent::codes::TIMEOUT`, `NOT_LISTENING`,
`ACCESS_DENIED_WINDOWS`/`_UNIX`, ...), not string literals typed at each
call site - a typo in a literal ("hadshake_failed") would silently
produce an untranslatable code no test could catch; a typo in a constant
name is a compile error. `agent_export_bundle`'s own `Result<_, String>`
still compiles unchanged via `impl From<AgentError> for String` (falls
back to `technical_detail`) - it is a different Rust command, not part
of this ADR's connection-error scope.

`race_cancellable` (Phase 3's own extracted, directly-testable
cancellation race) is now generic over the error type instead of
hard-coded to `String`, taking the "cancelled" value as a parameter -
`agent_get` passes `cancelled_error()` (`code: "cancelled"`) instead of
the old bare `"__cancelled__"` string sentinel `isCancelledRejection`
used to match on. Both existing `race_cancellable` tests (from Phase 3's
`std::future::pending()` proof) updated and still pass, unchanged in
what they prove.

    $ cargo build --lib && cargo test --lib && cargo clippy --lib && cargo fmt --check
    12 tests passed, 0 clippy warnings, fmt clean

A new `agent.rs` test (`all_codes_have_no_duplicates_and_match_the_expected_count`)
asserts `codes::ALL` has no duplicate and the exact count 12 - the
counterpart to `desktop/src/i18n/agentErrorCatalogue.ts`'s own hand-kept
`EXPECTED_CODES` list, which its own test checks the catalogue against.
Two lists, not a generated cross-language bridge (unlike kinds.json/
rules.json's real AST-derived generators) - documented as a lighter,
proportionate guarantee for a small, stable set of twelve strings
defined in one function, not forty-nine kinds or nineteen-plus rules
that genuinely grow over time.

## 2. Frontend: agentErrorCatalogue.ts, tauri.ts, useRecord.ts, SourceBanner.tsx

`desktop/src/i18n/agentErrorCatalogue.ts`: `{en, ar}` templates for all
12 codes, `{param}` interpolation, `messageFor` returning `{message,
known}` - `known: false` (a code this build predates) falls back to
`technical_detail` itself, tagged unknown, per the ADR's existing
fallback rule applied to this new content.

`tauri.ts`'s `isCancelledRejection` now checks `e.code === "cancelled"`
on the plain object Tauri rejects a command's promise with (confirmed:
Tauri serialises `Err(AgentError)` as that struct directly, not wrapped
in a JS `Error`) instead of the old bare-string equality check.
`isAgentErrorPayload` (exported) is the one place that object shape is
checked, reused by `useRecord.ts` rather than duplicated.

`Record.error`'s type widened from `string` to `string | AgentErrorPayload`
- the two existing sentinels (`"shell_required"`, `"no_bundle"`) and a
bundle-open failure (a different Rust command, unaffected, still a plain
string) stay strings; a live-poll connection failure is now the
structured object, checked with `isAgentErrorPayload` at the one place
it is caught (`useRecord.ts`'s live-poll catch block) rather than
coerced through `String(e)`, which would have produced the useless
`"[object Object]"` for this exact case.

`SourceBanner.tsx`: the primary message is now `messageFor(...).message`
- a real translated sentence, not the whole rejection wrapped in one
`TechnicalValue` span regardless of what it actually was (the ADR's
named complaint). `technical_detail` is kept, not discarded, behind a
native `<details>`/`<summary>` - "available on demand" read literally,
collapsed by default - shown only when `known` is true, so an
unrecognised code (whose "translated" message already *is*
`technical_detail`) does not show the identical sentence twice.

## 3. Go capability reason (internal/registry, cmd/netrewindd, internal/api/v1)

`registry.Snapshot` gains `ReasonCode`/`ReasonParams` (`omitempty`),
additive - `capabilitiesResponse` and `bundle.Manifest` both already
embed `registry.Snapshot` directly (confirmed by reading both files
before assuming), so `/v1/capabilities` and every evidence bundle get
the new fields with no other server-side code change at all.
`Registry.DownCoded`/`UnsupportedCoded` are new methods alongside the
unchanged `Down`/`Unsupported` - existing callers, existing bundle
fixtures, and every already-passing test are untouched.

Only the two call sites with a genuinely closed, small reason space were
converted (`cmd/netrewindd/main.go`): `"requires "+platform` (now
`registry.ReasonRequiresPlatform` with a `platform` param) and the
"stopped after Run() returned cleanly" case (now
`registry.ReasonCollectorStopped`). The third site - `err.Error()` for a
collector that failed with its own arbitrary internal error - is
deliberately left uncoded: a collector's startup error has no closed set
of translatable codes without a much larger refactor across every
collector package (`internal/collect/*`), which this slice does not
attempt. It keeps working exactly as before, falling back to showing the
raw reason - the same "no code, no crash, show the original" path an
older recorder or an unrecognised code already takes.

    $ gofmt -l cmd internal && go build ./... && go vet ./... && CGO_ENABLED=0 go test ./...
    all clean

New tests: `registry_test.go` proves `DownCoded`/`UnsupportedCoded`
populate the fields, proves the plain `Down`/`Unsupported` path leaves
them empty (not a zero-value placeholder), and proves `omitempty`
actually omits them from JSON rather than emitting empty strings/null.
`server_test.go`'s `TestCapabilitiesIncludesTheStructuredReasonWhenCoded`/
`TestCapabilitiesOmitsTheReasonCodeFieldsWhenThereIsNone` prove the same
two facts survive the real HTTP/JSON round trip, mirroring
`TestRulesIncludesTranslationsWhenTheRuleHasThem`'s pattern from the
first Phase 4 slice.

`ReasonCodesUsedByThisBuild` (new, in `registry.go`) plus a test proving
it has no duplicate and the expected count is the same
proportionate-not-generated pairing `agent.rs`'s `codes::ALL` uses,
matched against `desktop/src/i18n/capabilityReasonCatalogue.test.ts`'s
own hand-kept `EXPECTED_CODES` list.

## 4. Frontend: capabilityReasonCatalogue.ts, CapabilityTable.tsx

`desktop/src/i18n/capabilityReasonCatalogue.ts`: the same `{message,
known}` shape as `agentErrorCatalogue.ts`, two codes. `CapabilityTable.tsx`
- named in the execution order as wrapping `c.reason` in `ltr-field`,
"which forces monospace/LTR onto what is usually an English sentence" -
now shows the translated sentence as normal text, with the raw `reason`
behind a collapsed `<details>` exactly like `SourceBanner.tsx`'s new
pattern, only when there is an actual translation to disclose it behind.

An unsupported collector's reason was already never shown at all before
this change (`c.status !== "unsupported"` in the existing render
condition, backed by an existing, unmodified test:
"an unsupported collector's reason is the platform, not shown as a
failure") - that deliberate, pre-existing UX decision is unchanged. The
`requires_platform` code and its catalogue entry are still real and
correctly populated end to end (proven by the Go and TS tests above);
they simply have no current visible effect in `CapabilityTable.tsx`
today, which is this component's existing design, not a gap this slice
introduced or was asked to close.

## 5. Full regression

Rust: `cargo build --lib`, `cargo test --lib` (12 passed), `cargo clippy
--lib` (0 warnings), `cargo fmt --check` (clean).
Go: `gofmt -l cmd internal`, `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`,
`CGO_ENABLED=0 go test ./...` - all clean.
Frontend: `npx tsc --noEmit` clean; `npm test` - 9 files, 56 tests
passed (component tests for both `SourceBanner`'s and `CapabilityTable`'s
new coded-reason rendering, catalogue completeness/interpolation/
fallback tests for both new i18n modules); `npm run build` clean;
`npx playwright test` - 35 passed, unchanged from the previous slice
(this change touches error-path rendering, not the happy-path layout or
content those tests already cover).

## 6. What was not, and could not be, verified in this environment

Both halves of this slice only actually render with a real live
recorder connection: `SourceBanner`'s error banner needs a live-mode
connection failure, and `CapabilityTable` is not shown at all in demo
mode (`Overview.tsx`: `capabilities.length > 0`, and demo mode's
`record.capabilities` is always `[]` by design - confirmed by reading
`Overview.tsx` and ADR 0001's "Capability honesty in Demo mode" note).
No live recorder is reachable from this sandboxed environment. What was
verified instead, deliberately not overstated as more than it is:
- Rust: real unit tests of `AgentError` construction and the codes
  registry, `cargo test`, `cargo clippy`, `cargo fmt` all clean.
- Go: real unit and HTTP-handler tests of the registry and
  `/v1/capabilities`, including two tests that would fail if the new
  fields leaked into JSON when absent or were missing when present.
- Frontend: real React Testing Library component tests exercising the
  actual `SourceBanner`/`CapabilityTable` render output (text content,
  the `<details>` element's `open` attribute, DOM structure) against
  both a coded and an uncoded/unrecognised-code input - not merely
  "compiles" or "the catalogue function returns the right string in
  isolation."
This is real, load-bearing verification at every layer up to the actual
Tauri IPC round trip and a real socket/pipe failure, which needs the
compiled desktop app talking to an absent recorder - not achievable
here, and not claimed to have been checked.

## 7. Phase 4 status

Every item ADR 0004's Decision section named is now implemented: the
rule i18n contract and all 19 rules translated (first slice); the
event-kind catalogue (second slice); rules.json and real frontend
rendering of rule text (third slice); the untagged-Latin-sentence
scanner (fourth slice); Rust/Go structured error codes (this slice).
ADR 0004 updated to reflect this - Phase 4 is closed.

Still open, tracked separately, not part of ADR 0004: regenerating
demo-incidents.json so the per-clause `why` translation path
(implemented and unit-tested since the third slice) has real data to
render against in demo mode - the `internal/correlate/replaydemo`
investigation from evidence 38.
